[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-16", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 16 — CPU-bound in the join, not waiting; index-path twins take the row from 62 to 256 ops/s (511 at 32 slots)", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-16.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 16 - the SUT was CPU-bound in the Datalog join, not waiting; index-path twins take the row from 62 to 256 ops/s (511 at 32 slots)\n\n> Seed: iteration 15's S4 - at the 63 ops/s ceiling the SUT JVM showed\n> 284% CPU with 8 threads on 10 cores; predicted the handler threads\n> BLOCKED/WAITING on shared state. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2, rung\n> SF1 subset, origin :cross-host (simeon), mix IC02/07/08/09.\n\n## Generate\n\n  H-S4 (as seeded): most handler samples BLOCKED/WAITING on one monitor\n  or a CAS retry, not RUNNABLE in datalog.\n  Measurement: 8 x `jcmd Thread.print` at 3 s spacing during a 45 s run at\n  rate 64 / inflight 8, plus `ps %cpu` sampled at each dump.\n\n## Rank\n\n  64 handler samples: 52 RUNNABLE (busy), 12 WAITING (pool idle), 0 BLOCKED.\n  Busy by op: ic09 37, ic08 13, ic07 1, ic02 1.\n  Inclusive: datalog.core/q 42, hash-join-rows 35, scan* 17, query 16.\n  JVM CPU during the run: 739 / 446 / 726 / 594 / 729 / 621 / 661 / 589 %.\n\n  H-S4 REFUTED. The 284% of iteration 15 was `ps -o %cpu` read once after\n  the run -- on macOS that is the process's LIFETIME average, not load.\n  Sampled during the run the JVM uses 4.5-7.4 of 10 cores with 8 threads:\n  the SUT is CPU-bound in the general join, 71% of it IC09, 25% IC08, 67%\n  inside hash-join-rows (broad scan + group-by index per query).\n\n## Evolve - what changed, and the second measurement\n\n  The four reads written a second time against the datom plane's index\n  paths (entity = EAVT point lookup, datoms :avet [a v] = AVET point\n  lookup) as twins of the Datalog versions -- ADR-2608039970's direct L1\n  binding, a legitimate surface. Served as `:impl :index` only when all\n  392 official parameters (99 x 4) answer identically both ways at\n  startup (`index-path-agrees?`), else the index path is a 409 and the\n  Datalog path keeps serving.\n\n  Gate shown both ways: an ascending-order IC02 twin -> 47 disagreements,\n  409; unbroken -> 392/392, 200. A top-19 IC08 twin was NOT caught on the\n  3,500-post subset: no answer there reaches 20 rows, so `take 19` equals\n  `take 20` -- the boundary the control needed did not exist in that\n  data. Stated, not hidden.\n\n  Prediction before the run: IC09 and IC08 p50 drop >= 5x; the 8-slot\n  ceiling moves from ~64 to >= 256 ops/s; the Datalog arm re-measured on\n  the restarted SUT stays ~64.\n\n    arm                 rate  on-time%  thru/s   ic02   ic07   ic08   ic09 (p50 ms)\n    datalog, 8 slots      64   100.00    62.4    17.2   15.1  128.1  292.7   <- reference, as iteration 15\n    datalog, 8 slots      96    14.95    65.2\n    index,   8 slots     256   100.00   255.6    10.3    9.9    9.7   41.2   <- 4.1x\n    index,   8 slots     384    51.81   354.1\n    index,  32 slots     512   100.00   511.1    12.1   11.3   10.9   55.9   <- 8.2x\n    index,  32 slots     768    21.71   618.2\n\n  Held: IC09 7.1x, IC08 13x; IC02/IC07 fell to ~10 ms, which is the\n  Tailscale rtt -- the 8-slot ceiling is now the wire, so 32 slots were\n  run as their own column.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - The price of the general join for these two shapes is 4-13x per\n    operation, and it is CPU, not waiting. hash-join-rows builds a\n    group-by index over the broad relation per query when the budget\n    says so; for IC09's `[?msg hasCreator ?f2]` with thousands of\n    friend-of-friend keys that is a group-by over 86,932 rows every call.\n  - The remaining IC09 cost (41-56 ms) is answer size: friend-of-friend x\n    messages materialised, then the top 20 by date. The hot db's avet is\n    a hash map, so there is no ordered walk by creationDate to stop at 20;\n    a sorted value index is the next lever, and it is an engine change.\n  - Ratio to the audited world No.1 (139,411 at SF300): 545 at 8 slots,\n    273 at 32 -- raw, and SF1 subset is not on the table.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH (62.4 -> 255.6 with rung,\norigin, mix and inflight held; 511.1 in the 32-slot column). Recorded.\n\nSeeds for iteration 17, in order:\n\n  - S5 (engine): make the Datalog path pay what the index path pays. The\n    dumps name the spot: hash-join-rows on the bound-key clause. Either\n    the planner routes a clause whose bound column is the avet key\n    straight to by-predicate-value (no broad scan, no group-by), or the\n    budget accounts the group-by cost. Predicted: Datalog IC08 -> ~10 ms,\n    IC09 -> ~60 ms, Datalog arm to ~200 ops/s at 8 slots.\n  - S6 (top-k): an ordered value index (sorted avet, or a per-attribute\n    sorted vector rebuilt on commit) so IC02/IC09 walk creationDate\n    descending and stop at 20. Predicted: IC09 -> ~15 ms.\n  - S2 (rung): full SF1 on simeon with the index path (the load fits;\n    IC09 index on full SF1 is bounded by answer size, not by the join).\n  - S3 (mix): short reads IS1-IS7 on the index path.\n"}]
